Computercraft-Legacy
===================

This distribution is a modified version of ComputerCraft for Minecraft 1.7.10.
It is distributed under the ComputerCraft Public License (CCPL); the full text
is in LICENSE. This file records where the code came from, because that is not
recoverable from the license files alone.


1. Provenance of the ComputerCraft code
---------------------------------------

The ComputerCraft source code for Minecraft 1.7.10 was never published.
Public ComputerCraft source begins with the Minecraft 1.8 line: the oldest
tag in dan200/ComputerCraft is 1.79, which is the Minecraft 1.8.9 build.
No 1.7.10 source tree has been released by the original authors.

The ComputerCraft code in this project was therefore recovered by decompiling
the Minecraft 1.7.10 binary distribution of ComputerCraft 1.75, which was the
last ComputerCraft release for Minecraft 1.7.10. This is corroborated by the
bundled in-game changelog, whose most recent entry is "New Features in
ComputerCraft 1.75"; no later version appears anywhere in the ROM.

That decompilation is expressly permitted by the license under which
ComputerCraft was distributed. CCPL section 0 defines a "User" as anybody
that interacts with the software in one of the following ways, and lists
"decompile" among them. CCPL section 4 states:

    "The user has the right to decompile the source code, look at either
     the decompiled version or the original source code, and to modify it."

All recovered ComputerCraft code, and all ComputerCraft assets, remain
licensed under the CCPL. CCPL section 5 requires this:

    "All distributions of this mod must remain licensed under the CCPL."

Because the ComputerCraft 1.7.10 source was never published, no upstream
file exists to diff against. Provenance of individual files cannot be
established by comparison and is instead recorded by convention: files
carrying no other notice are decompiled ComputerCraft 1.7.10 code under
the CCPL.


2. Code that is not ComputerCraft 1.7.10 code
--------------------------------------------

This project also incorporates code backported from CC: Tweaked
(https://github.com/cc-tweaked/CC-Tweaked), a fork of ComputerCraft
originally maintained by SquidDev and now under the cc-tweaked organisation.
That backport is substantial, and predates the Minecraft
1.7.10 code it is layered onto. In outline it covers:

  - the Cobalt Lua runtime integration, in
    dan200.computercraft.core.lua.lib.cobalt and
    dan200.computercraft.core.lua.binfs
  - the extended Lua API surface in dan200.computercraft.api.lua, which
    includes interfaces that do not exist in ComputerCraft 1.7.10 at all
    (ILuaAPI, IArguments, ArgumentDelegator, IBinaryHandler, ILuaEnvironment,
    IExtendedLuaObject, IExtendedLuaTask, ILuaAPIFactory,
    ILuaObjectWithArguments, IPeripheralWithArguments, MethodResult,
    ILuaCallback)
  - the Lua library and delayed-task runtime in
    dan200.computercraft.core.lua.lib (DelayedTask, DelayedTasks,
    LuaEnvironment, LuaHelpers, BinaryConverter)
  - the computer and peripheral registry architecture in
    dan200.computercraft.shared.computer.core (ComputerRegistry,
    ClientComputerRegistry, ServerComputerRegistry, ComputerFamily,
    TileComputerBase) and its peripherals, which the 1.7.10 code does not
    use, and which coexist in this tree with the 1.7.10-era
    ComputerPeripheral / TileWirelessModem / WirelessModemPeripheral code
  - backported Lua ROM modules under
    assets/computercraft/lua/rom/modules/main/cc, which carry
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 headers
  - the backported ROM programs assets/computercraft/lua/rom/programs/{about,
    motd} and .../programs/{fun/speaker,pocket/equip,pocket/unequip}, which
    likewise carry SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 headers (the remaining ROM
    programs are CCPL, as decompiled from 1.75)
  - the ROM module assets/computercraft/lua/rom/modules/main/cc/internal/
    event.lua, ported from CC: Tweaked's cc/internal/event.lua (MPL-2.0;
    the file states this in its own header). It is loaded by rom/programs/help
    to discard the char event left behind by a keypress.
  - file transfer (Batch J), ported from CC: Tweaked 1.120.2:
      * dan200.computercraft.core.apis.transfer.TransferredFiles and
        TransferredFile, from cc/core/src/main/java/.../apis/transfer
      * dan200.computercraft.shared.computer.upload.FileUpload, FileSlice,
        UploadResult and UploadState, from cc/common/src/main/java/...
        /computer/upload (UploadState factors out the accumulation logic that
        upstream keeps in ServerInputState)
      * dan200.computercraft.shared.network.FileUploadPacket, from
        cc/common/src/main/java/.../network/server/UploadFileMessage, with its
        own codec because the legacy packet format cannot express it
      * dan200.computercraft.shared.computer.upload.ServerFileUploadHandler,
        from the upload half of ServerInputState
      * the ROM programs assets/computercraft/lua/rom/programs/import and
        assets/computercraft/lua/rom/modules/main/cc/internal/import.lua,
        which carry SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0 headers
      * the file_transfer branch of assets/computercraft/lua/rom/programs/shell
    The rest of that feature - the SDL3 drop watcher, the size/authorisation
    checks, IContainerComputer and IComputerGui - is this project's own code.
  - the full wired modem (Batch N), ported from CC: Tweaked 1.120.2's
    WiredModemFullBlock, WiredModemFullBlockEntity, WiredModemLocalPeripheral,
    WiredModemPeripheral and ModemState:
      * dan200.computercraft.shared.peripheral.modem.BlockWiredModemFull,
        ItemWiredModemFull, TileWiredModemFull, WiredModemLocalPeripheral and
        ModemState
      * the per-peripheral remote-wrapper split in TileCable.Peripheral, which
        gives each attached face its own wrapper set and its own computer
      * the shared ModemState behaviour in ModemPeripheral, so all six faces of
        the full modem see the same open channels
    The 1.7.10 implementation is a new port against TileCable/INetwork rather
    than a copy of the 1.20 code, but the feature and its name/ID/NBT semantics
    are derived from the upstream classes listed above.

  - one deliberate behavioural divergence, in this project's own code: the
    `upgrade` guard at the head of `PocketAPI`'s `pocket.equipBack()`
    (dan200.computercraft.shared.pocket.apis.PocketAPI). ComputerCraft 1.7.10
    refused to equip only when the stored id was 1 (its one upgrade); the early
    port here refused when the id was one of 1-6. Both let an addon's own
    upgrade id in the same NBT key be overwritten, which orphans the peripheral
    that upgrade had attached, because `pocket.unequipBack()` can only give back
    ids it knows. The guard now refuses whenever the key is present. No
    ComputerCraft-only world is affected, since every writer of that key in
    this mod goes through PocketUpgrades. No upstream code is involved.

  - the turtle refuel handler registry, ported from CC: Tweaked 1.120.2:
      * dan200.computercraft.api.turtle.TurtleRefuelHandler
      * dan200.computercraft.impl.TurtleRefuelHandlers
      * dan200.computercraft.shared.turtle.core.FurnaceRefuelHandler
      * the delegation in TurtleRefuelCommand, which now asks those handlers
        instead of testing furnace burn time itself
    The 1.7.10 port keeps the upstream contract -- an OptionalInt where empty
    means "not my fuel" and of(0) means "mine, but the limit was zero" -- and
    its guards against handlers that consume during the zero-limit probe or
    return a negative yield. The IC2 and CoFH energy handlers in
    dan200.computercraft.shared.turtle.refuel are this project's own code, using
    the same EU/RF-per-fuel ratios as CCTweaks' ITurtleFuelProvider
    integrations so a pack migrating off CCTweaks keeps its economy. IC2 and
    CoFH Core remain compile-only dependencies; nothing from them is copied or
    shipped, and their API classes are only resolved when those mods are loaded.

LWJGL 3 (including lwjgl-sdl) is a compile-time dependency only. It is not
bundled or shaded: lwjgl3ify provides it at runtime, and the mod declares
lwjgl3ify as a required dependency. LWJGL is BSD-3-Clause.

lwjgl3ify itself (LGPL-3.0) is likewise compile-time only, and only for its
@Lwjgl3Aware marker annotation, which tells that mod not to rewrite this
project's LWJGL3 references. No lwjgl3ify code is copied, linked or shipped.

Files sourced from CC: Tweaked are licensed under the Mozilla Public
License 2.0. The full text is in LICENSE-MPL-2.0. MPL-2.0 is a
file-level copyleft: those files remain MPL-2.0, and only those files.

