3. Bundled third-party libraries
--------------------------------

The following libraries are shaded into the released jar. Cobalt,
Java-WebSocket and slf4j-api are MIT licensed; the JVM Downgrader API stubs
are under the LGPL 2.1. Each is redistributed with its license text in
LICENSE-Cobalt, LICENSE-Java-WebSocket, LICENSE-slf4j-api and
LICENSE-jvmdowngrader respectively.

  Cobalt 0.9.9               MIT    LICENSE-Cobalt
      https://github.com/cc-tweaked/Cobalt
      A re-entrant fork of LuaJ, used as the Lua 5.1/5.2 runtime. Published
      under the cc.tweaked Maven coordinate; earlier releases were published
      as org.squiddev:Cobalt.
      Copyright Luaj.org (2009-2011) and SquidDev (2015-2020).

  Java-WebSocket 1.5.6       MIT    LICENSE-Java-WebSocket
      https://github.com/TooTallNate/Java-WebSocket
      Copyright Nathan Rajlich (2010-2020).

  slf4j-api 2.0.6            MIT    LICENSE-slf4j-api
      https://www.slf4j.org
      Copyright QOS.ch Sarl, Switzerland (2004-2022).
      A transitive dependency of Java-WebSocket. It is not declared in
      dependencies.gradle, but it is present in the released jar.

  JVM Downgrader API stubs   LGPL-2.1   LICENSE-jvmdowngrader
      https://github.com/unimined/JvmDowngrader
      Copyright (C) 2024 William Gray ("Wagyourtail").
      Shaded by jvmDowngrader when jvmDowngraderStubsProvider = shade, as a
      minimised set: only the stubs the downgraded bytecode actually calls
      are copied, relocated under dan200.computercraft.shadow, so they
      cannot collide with an unrelocated copy supplied by another mod.

      The stubs re-implement Java 9+ API entry points that have no Java 8
      equivalent. Only two classes are actually packed in for this code base:
      J_L_Record, which becomes the supertype of Cobalt's eight record classes
      (java.lang.Record does not exist on Java 8), and J_U_Arrays plus its
      ArraySupport helper, which back Arrays.mismatch and
      Arrays.equals(byte[],int,int,byte[],int,int) in Cobalt's LuaString
      comparison. Everything else the downgrader needed was inlined into the
      calling class instead -- for example Objects.requireNonNullElse becomes a
      private jvmdg$inlined$requireNonNullElse helper.

      A J_L_Deprecated marker annotation is also written into
      RuntimeVisibleAnnotations on seven classes, as a stand-in for
      java.lang.Deprecated. The minimiser does not copy it into the jar. That is
      harmless: the JVM ignores an annotation whose type cannot be resolved, and
      getAnnotations() and isAnnotationPresent(Deprecated.class) are unaffected
      (verified against the packaged jar on Java 25).

      JvmDowngrader licenses its API stubs under LGPL-2.1 with a commercial
      alternative, and its own LICENSE.md takes the position that shading
      these classes into an application is a "combined work" that does not
      force the application onto the GPL, provided the unshaded or
      undowngraded artifact (or its source) is made available. This project
      is not commercial and publishes its complete source, so that condition
      is met. Note that the LGPL-2.1 text shipped here is JvmDowngrader's own
      license file; the stub class contents are derived from OpenJDK, whose
      provenance JvmDowngrader does not restate per file. That question is
      recorded here as open, in the same way as the ComputerCraft API notice
      discussed in section 2.

      Choosing 'shade' over 'gtnhlib' is deliberate. GTNHLib does not ship
      these stubs: it downloads jvmdowngrader-java-api at runtime from
      maven.wagyourtail.xyz through its dependency loader, and its
      system-classloader mirroring is skipped whenever the system class
      loader is not a URLClassLoader, which is always the case on Java 9+.
      This mod requires Java 17+ via lwjgl3ify, so that route would put a
      network fetch and a class loader accident in the middle of
      LuaString.compareTo. Shading costs roughly 5 KB and makes the jar
      self-contained.

ComputerCraft 1.7.10 embedded LuaJ 2.0.3. This project does not. The
previous LICENSE-luaj has therefore been removed, and replaced by
LICENSE-Cobalt. Cobalt is itself a fork of LuaJ, which is why the LuaJ
copyright is carried in LICENSE-Cobalt.


4. Assets
---------

Textures, language files, the Lua ROM and the help text are ComputerCraft
assets and are covered by the CCPL. The in-game licenses listing at
assets/computercraft/lua/rom/help/licenses/ is retained from ComputerCraft
and does not describe this project's dependencies.

The demonstration programs under
assets/computercraft/lua/treasure/ are the work of third-party contributors
rather than of ComputerCraft's authors: nitrogenfingers, GopherAtl, Lyqyd,
TheOriginalBIT, fredthead, GravityScore, JTK, vilsol and others. They are
redistributed here with the rest of the mod, under the CCPL.


